{"kind":"report","text":"Technical advice on db7d79e97, not Source approval. I inspected partition-proof.ts, receiver-reads.ts, receiver-facts.ts, native-birth.ts, the pure partition provenance bounds, proof-byte/preparation admission and existing focused witness descriptions. No Source edits or new test runs.\n\nUse a 32-call/32-distinct-frame TOTAL admission allowance as the first candidate, with the existing depth4 and one original10s deadline. That accommodates the two stated17-frame cases. A deepest receiver closure14 plus a disjoint deepest current-source closure15 suggests29 frames for one such foreign source; that is a calculation to witness, not measured proof. Do not choose64 merely as spare capacity: demonstrate the legitimate multi-source case that needs it, and budget its additional producer preparation/parse work. Neither32 nor64 guarantees every possible many-fact act fits; exceeding the published resource envelope remains unavailable, never an authority prohibition on cross-room relationships.\n\nSeparate total collection work from a single identity proof. Currently Collection.finish() returns the entire operation cache and source()/receiver() pass that to the pure policy; PARTITION_IDENTITY_BOUNDS still accepts16 total frames. Increasing only collector calls/frames will still reject a17-frame union. Keep the existing finite per-identity chain bound and pass only the exact dependency closure for the receiver/source/bootstrap/publication question being checked. Do not pass every unrelated frame accumulated earlier. Shared raw reads remain charged to one admission-wide account, deduplicated by full ScopeRef/incarnation/seq/hash; a per-question closure is not a new allowance. A positive check still verifies every required cause/send/ordinal/pin/sibling relation. No semantic inference from a label or arbitrary caller mode.\n\nConcrete byte accounting: reserve at most1MiB per physical RPC before starting it, so32 calls permit at most32MiB reserved work; separately retain the current16MiB ceiling on actual unique admitted UTF-8 proof bytes across the WHOLE operation, charged before parse. Duplicate cached frames add no actual bytes, but any repeated physical read still spends a call and reservation. Bad/missing replies spend work too. Keep the existing per-frame sender-side admission and NativeBirth preparation/definition bounds; their finite per-call costs are multiplied by the total RPC bound and must be reported, not treated as free metadata. The global collection and per-identity constants need distinct names/meanings.\n\nAvoid an eviction/refetch scheme solely to fix the17-frame problem. First use a bounded operation-local raw store plus minimal closure views. Release duplicate textual/decoded representations when no longer needed, but do not refund total work or forget authenticated position conflicts. An ephemeral validation memo can be keyed by exact receiver policy/pin, full immutable refs and proof context; it must not become retained or cross-admission derived identity. If raw eviction is needed for actual memory, later refetches remain charged, and every identity judgment still needs its complete authenticated closure. Never cache an unqualified upgraded under label.\n\nBound outstanding materializations too: one physical read at a time is a simple initial option; if parallelism is needed, reserve an explicit small pending slot/byte allowance before I/O and retain it until physical settlement. A logical timeout does not prove cancellation or drained capacity. Stop the admission at the original deadline, check it after synchronous work, and never apply a late proof. Do not claim synchronous JSON parsing is preemptible.\n\nThe32MiB reservation is NOT peak memory, and even16MiB actual UTF-8 is not a16MiB heap bound. Account for wire strings, UTF-8 buffers, parsed entries, NativeBirth's retained proof strings, temporary canonicalization and pending replies. Show a near-bound memory witness under the actual runtime before claiming a safe peak; lower the actual/resident byte ceiling or compact representations if it does not fit. No larger raw-memory allowance is approved by this advice.\n\nMinimum witnesses before first Source:\n1. Actual native two-root direct receiver plus current foreign lane, with the existing authorized relationship and later matching release/restart; and the stated deep/same-root17-frame composition. Assert real total RPC/frame counters. Restoring the16-call or whole-cache-as-identity-proof mistake must distinguish these cases. Add the deepest disjoint case if the supported depth4 envelope claims it.\n2. Concurrent repeated receiver/source/fact requests share exact reads; distinct requests exhaust the TOTAL allowance, with no33rd physical RPC or reset at facts/bootstrap/publication. Wrong hash/incarnation, altered creator/send and cycle/depth overflow cannot be repaired by another cached label.\n3. Byte/frame/work/deadline boundaries, including actual native pre-materialization refusal, missing/oversized reply, late settlement and pending/resident memory. Exhaustion returns unresolved/unavailable with no inferred origin or successful admission; disclose CPU/clock/transport stand-ins.\n4. Real runtime delivery/driver/publication and per-receiving-run replay use the same bounded policy; mixed legacy/new receivers and both supply orders preserve raw retention and old4/5 identity. No global mutable callback or upgrade cache. These are the already owed integration boundaries, not a request to repeat whole suites.\n\nThis is a concrete finite strategy and witness floor. It does not approve the unfinished directory head, alter old cohorts/default Source, impose same-room role restrictions, or authorize deployment/provider work.","body":{"formal_approval":"false","no_git_artifact":"true","promise":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:16afba0f6b17ce5c18b7aede519c528785fb5182","request":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3